# Triggers a per-device systemd service (usb-automount@.service) for
# every USB-attached block device PARTITION that appears - covers
# ordinary USB flash drives (sda1, sdb2, ... - the common case for
# this project's own rescue/installer use). Deliberately matches
# partitions (trailing digit), not whole disks - mounting a raw,
# unpartitioned disk isn't something this project's own file-browsing/
# auto-select logic expects to find directly.
#
# SUBSYSTEMS=="usb" (plural - walks the device's own ANCESTOR chain,
# not just the device's own immediate ENV{ID_BUS} property) rather
# than ENV{ID_BUS}=="usb" - found via a real report: a USB-attached
# SSD using a USB-to-SCSI/UAS bridge chip presents its block device
# under the "scsi" subsystem directly, with ID_BUS often left unset or
# not reporting "usb" for that immediate device at all, even though a
# USB device genuinely sits further up its own device tree. Dropping
# the USB check entirely (the first workaround tried) fixed that case
# but then also matched a genuinely internal SATA drive, which has no
# USB ancestor anywhere in its own tree at all - SUBSYSTEMS=="usb"
# correctly distinguishes both cases at once, and is itself the same,
# standard idiom systemd's own real udev rules use for this exact
# purpose (confirmed directly in the real source, 60-persistent-
# storage.rules.in: "KERNEL==\"sd*[!0-9]|sr*\", SUBSYSTEMS==\"usb\",
# IMPORT{builtin}=\"usb_id\"" - practically the same match this rule
# itself makes).
#
# SYSTEMD_WANTS (not a direct RUN+= mount here) - lets systemd own the
# actual mount's lifecycle (start/stop, ordering, logging via the
# journal) rather than a bare, untracked udev RUN+= script, and keeps
# the actual mount logic in one place (the service's own ExecStart)
# instead of duplicated between a udev-invoked script and a service.
SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="sd[a-z][0-9]", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", ACTION=="add", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="usb-automount@%k.service"
